1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:05,444
Ask a class why people marry, and you will hear about love, family and happiness.

2
00:00:05,444 --> 00:00:09,689
A sociologist asks a different question: what is a marriage?

3
00:00:09,689 --> 00:00:10,702
Two people, yes.

4
00:00:10,933 --> 00:00:15,414
But Steven Nock's answer is that a marriage is never just two people.

5
00:00:15,414 --> 00:00:23,643
It comes with rules, expectations and helpers already attached: law, religion, custom, relatives.

6
00:00:23,643 --> 00:00:29,433
They existed before the couple met, and they limit the couple, but they also protect and guide them.

7
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:42,995
Welcome to Class 12 English, Unit 14: Marriage as a Social Institution, by the American sociologist Steven Nock.

8
00:00:45,367 --> 00:00:46,475
Six parts.

9
00:00:46,475 --> 00:00:48,494
Nock and the essay's argument.

10
00:00:48,494 --> 00:00:51,020
An institution with soft boundaries.

11
00:00:51,020 --> 00:00:53,520
Durkheim and the limits on desire.

12
00:00:53,520 --> 00:00:55,681
Marriage against cohabitation.

13
00:00:55,681 --> 00:00:57,725
Marriage as social capital.

14
00:00:57,725 --> 00:01:00,580
And normative marriage, in six dimensions.

15
00:01:04,933 --> 00:01:12,565
Steven Nock was a professor of sociology at the University of Virginia who studied marriage, cohabitation and divorce.

16
00:01:12,565 --> 00:01:19,062
His book Marriage in Men's Lives asks why married men behave differently from unmarried men.

17
00:01:19,062 --> 00:01:21,653
This passage is from its second chapter.

18
00:01:21,653 --> 00:01:27,070
It is the most academic essay in your book, but it contains only five ideas.

19
00:01:27,070 --> 00:01:34,804
One warning: the textbook's headnote talks of a national marriage debate and programmes; none of that is in the passage.

20
00:01:34,804 --> 00:01:36,292
Use the passage itself.

21
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:40,444
Here is the argument in five steps.

22
00:01:40,444 --> 00:01:43,192
One: marriage is an institution.

23
00:01:43,192 --> 00:01:47,007
Two: it benefits men by setting limits on desire.

24
00:01:47,007 --> 00:01:51,186
Three: unlike cohabitation, it supplies a template.

25
00:01:51,186 --> 00:01:53,999
Four: it is a form of social capital.

26
00:01:53,999 --> 00:01:57,463
Five: normative marriage has six dimensions.

27
00:01:57,463 --> 00:02:00,758
Learn these five and you can answer any question on the essay.

28
00:02:05,533 --> 00:02:11,706
Nock's first sentence is his thesis: a marriage is much more than the sum of two spouses.

29
00:02:11,706 --> 00:02:17,045
It is a relationship defined by legal, moral and conventional assumptions.

30
00:02:17,045 --> 00:02:30,287
It is an institution, in the sociologist's sense: an established pattern of rules and roles, like the family, education or the economy, culturally patterned and tied to those other institutions.

31
00:02:30,287 --> 00:02:39,467
Its rules come from outside any particular couple, and couples inherit them, as heirs to a vast system of understood principles.

32
00:02:40,700 --> 00:02:50,715
Those rules set what Nock calls soft boundaries round the couple: the commonly understood limits of behaviour that separate marriage from every other kind of relationship.

33
00:02:50,715 --> 00:02:54,152
Soft, because nobody is arrested for crossing them.

34
00:02:54,152 --> 00:02:58,018
Opinion, custom and law enforce them together, and they bend.

35
00:03:02,800 --> 00:03:04,520
Why does marriage matter to men?

36
00:03:04,520 --> 00:03:09,611
Nock borrows an old argument from the French sociologist Émile Durkheim.

37
00:03:09,611 --> 00:03:17,489
In modern societies basic needs are mostly met, so desire moves on: to comfort, luxury, prestige.

38
00:03:17,489 --> 00:03:22,372
And those wants have no natural limit; satisfied, they demand more.

39
00:03:22,372 --> 00:03:29,182
So the central problem is setting legitimate limits on desire, and Durkheim says marriage does it.

40
00:03:29,182 --> 00:03:40,115
It is an organ of society that ties a man to one partner, gives his need for love a strictly definite object, closes the horizon, and so gives him moral equilibrium.

41
00:03:41,333 --> 00:03:43,418
Equilibrium means balance.

42
00:03:43,418 --> 00:03:49,342
A person who wants everything is always off balance, because there is always something more to want.

43
00:03:49,342 --> 00:03:51,751
Limits give a fixed centre to live from.

44
00:03:51,751 --> 00:04:00,728
In plain words: a man who knows what is expected of him, and what is closed to him, is calmer than a man who is free to want anything.

45
00:04:05,500 --> 00:04:11,179
Then an objection: surely two people can live happily together without marrying?

46
00:04:11,179 --> 00:04:21,257
Cohabiting couples, Nock agrees, have tremendous freedom: how to deal with each other's parents, whether to holiday together, whether to pool money.

47
00:04:21,257 --> 00:04:25,723
Everything is theirs to invent, and their parents get little guidance too.

48
00:04:25,723 --> 00:04:33,405
Married couples face many of the same decisions, but they have a pattern to follow: marriage supplies a template.

49
00:04:33,405 --> 00:04:39,027
Notice the double edge: the cohabiting couple's freedom is exactly the absence of help.

50
00:04:40,233 --> 00:04:42,969
One sentence shows the difference.

51
00:04:42,969 --> 00:04:58,646
I would like you to meet my wife says a great deal: pledges of faithfulness, duties to children and relatives, and these are not just personal promises but enforceable, because others expect them and law, religion and custom back them up.

52
00:04:58,646 --> 00:05:02,097
Now finish this one: I would like you to meet my, what?

53
00:05:02,097 --> 00:05:03,516
There is not even a word.

54
00:05:03,516 --> 00:05:09,258
That missing word, Nock says, shows how little such relationships are governed by convention.

55
00:05:09,258 --> 00:05:10,522
It is not an institution.

56
00:05:15,300 --> 00:05:21,500
Step four: marriage is a form of capital, as surely as any other resource.

57
00:05:21,500 --> 00:05:25,914
Nock borrows three kinds from the sociologist James Coleman.

58
00:05:25,914 --> 00:05:31,083
Physical capital: tools, buildings, money, wholly tangible.

59
00:05:31,083 --> 00:05:36,018
Human capital: the skills and knowledge in a person, less tangible.

60
00:05:36,018 --> 00:05:42,398
And social capital: found in the relations among persons, less tangible yet.

61
00:05:42,398 --> 00:05:47,803
It is a network of people linked by trust that obligations will be incurred and repaid.

62
00:05:49,033 --> 00:05:51,691
How does marriage create social capital?

63
00:05:51,691 --> 00:05:57,316
Through marriage, husband and wife become connected to new kin, and their friends.

64
00:05:57,316 --> 00:06:05,363
Those ties are woven with threads of obligations: in need you can call on relatives and expect help, and the debts persist.

65
00:06:05,363 --> 00:06:17,553
So the institution of marriage is an individual asset: intangible, but no less important than money, and harder to keep, because it exists only in relationships with others.

66
00:06:22,333 --> 00:06:28,780
Finally, the six dimensions that define normative marriage in America, as Nock describes it.

67
00:06:28,780 --> 00:06:30,355
Entered voluntarily.

68
00:06:30,355 --> 00:06:31,879
By mature adults.

69
00:06:31,879 --> 00:06:33,298
Heterosexual.

70
00:06:33,298 --> 00:06:35,824
With the husband as the principal earner.

71
00:06:35,824 --> 00:06:38,467
Sexually faithful on both sides.

72
00:06:38,467 --> 00:06:39,616
And leading to parenthood.

73
00:06:40,833 --> 00:06:42,059
Two cautions.

74
00:06:42,059 --> 00:06:51,499
Normative does not mean normal: it means how marriage should be, by a society's standard, and Nock admits many real marriages depart from it.

75
00:06:51,499 --> 00:07:00,079
Yet the ideal is still a form of social control, a force greater than the individuals, channelling their behaviour and hopes.

76
00:07:00,079 --> 00:07:04,650
And remember the context: this describes America in the nineteen nineties.

77
00:07:04,650 --> 00:07:08,448
The law has since changed, and Nepal's law and customs are different again.

78
00:07:10,867 --> 00:07:11,637
Pause and think.

79
00:07:18,933 --> 00:07:25,128
No template and no convention: there isn't even a word to finish "meet my ___".

80
00:07:27,100 --> 00:07:27,535
One more.

81
00:07:35,167 --> 00:07:41,422
In relationships bound by trust — which is why marriage, linking new kin, creates it.

82
00:07:43,600 --> 00:07:44,695
Quick recap.

83
00:07:44,695 --> 00:07:46,036
Nock's five steps.

84
00:07:46,036 --> 00:07:49,279
Marriage as an institution with soft boundaries.

85
00:07:49,279 --> 00:07:51,245
Durkheim's limits on desire.

86
00:07:51,245 --> 00:07:54,175
Cohabitation's freedom without a template.

87
00:07:54,175 --> 00:07:55,477
Social capital.

88
00:07:55,477 --> 00:07:58,303
And the six dimensions of normative marriage.

89
00:08:02,500 --> 00:08:05,093
That was Marriage as a Social Institution.

90
00:08:05,093 --> 00:08:09,572
Next essay: Knowledge and Wisdom, by Bertrand Russell.

91
00:08:09,572 --> 00:08:10,163
See you there.

